  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Spy satellites caught a 92-metre machine the CIA said couldn't fly. Kaspiysk harbour, on the Caspian Sea, late 1960s. Low over the water, in plain sight.",
        "visual_query": "soviet caspian sea harbour",
        "visual_fallback": "grey sea"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Ten jet engines. Five hundred and forty-four tonnes loaded. A wingspan barely a third of the hull's length.",
        "visual_query": "cold war reconnaissance satellite imagery",
        "visual_fallback": "archive film"
      },
      {
        "narration": "So how does half a thousand tonnes stay up on stubs like that? Analysts filed it as KASP-A and argued.",
        "visual_query": "caspian sea low aerial water",
        "visual_fallback": "open water"
      },
      {
        "narration": "It rode a cushion of its own trapped air, metres above the surface. A ground-effect craft, designed by Rostislav Alexeyev. Soviet engineers called it an ekranoplan.",
        "visual_query": "aircraft skimming low over sea",
        "visual_fallback": "low flight"
      },
      {
        "narration": "First flight, October 1966. For twenty-two years nothing bigger flew anywhere, until pilot error dropped it into the Caspian in 1980.",
        "visual_query": "soviet naval aviation archive footage",
        "visual_fallback": "rusting hull"
      }
    ],
